## Fluent Object Construction

```xiom
pub type ServerConfig = {
  host: Str;
  port: Int;
  timeout: Int;
  max_connections: Int;
  tls_enabled: Bool;
}

pub type ServerBuilder = {
  host: Str;
  port: Int;
  timeout: Int;
  max_connections: Int;
  tls_enabled: Bool;
}

impl ServerBuilder {
  fn new() -> ServerBuilder {
    return ServerBuilder{
      host: "localhost",
      port: 8080,
      timeout: 30,
      max_connections: 100,
      tls_enabled: false,
    };
  }

  fn host(self: &mut ServerBuilder, h: Str) -> &mut ServerBuilder {
    self.host = h;
    return self;
  }

  fn port(self: &mut ServerBuilder, p: Int) -> &mut ServerBuilder {
    self.port = p;
    return self;
  }

  fn build(self: &ServerBuilder) -> ServerConfig {
    return ServerConfig{
      host: self.host,
      port: self.port,
      timeout: self.timeout,
      max_connections: self.max_connections,
      tls_enabled: self.tls_enabled,
    };
  }
}

fn main() {
  let config = ServerBuilder::new()
    .host("0.0.0.0")
    .port(3000)
    .timeout(60)
    .build();
  // Only specified what we needed — rest uses defaults
}
```

### When to Use Builder

- Struct has 5+ fields
- Many fields have sensible defaults
- Construction needs validation (put it in build())
- You want a fluent, self-documenting API
- Fields depend on each other (e.g., port range depends on host type)

### Builder with Validation

```xiom
fn build(self: &ServerBuilder) -> Result[ServerConfig, Str] {
  if self.port == 0 { return Err("Port cannot be 0"); }
  if self.timeout < 0 { return Err("Timeout must be non-negative"); }
  return Ok(ServerConfig{ /* ... */ });
}
```

### Generic Builder

```xiom
fn configure_server[T](f: fn(&mut ServerBuilder) -> &mut ServerBuilder) -> ServerConfig {
  let mut builder = ServerBuilder::new();
  f(&mut builder);
  return builder.build();
}

let config = configure_server(|b| b.host("0.0.0.0").port(8080));
```

